WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.901
-So fun last night.
-Wasn't that a blast?

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.367
-Oh, my God.
-You are the coolest ever.

00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:05.701
-I was the coolest one
in the room.

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:07.734
-You were.
-No, I wasn't. I wasn't.

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:11.801
-Are you excited about this or
is it too much attention on you?

00:00:11.801 --> 00:00:13.367
-I'm excited about it.
It's a weird thing.

00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:15.067
Like, when you're up
for a Grammy or whatever,

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:16.334
it's like,
"Oh, that's so nice."

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:18.434
And then you start going,
"What if I don't win?

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:20.033
That's gonna be weird."
You know?

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:22.200
And so when I see my name
in the nominations,

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.367
I'm like, "That's so cool,

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.133
but, oh, gosh,
what if I don't get in?"

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.033
So it's a weird thing.
-This one you are winning.

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.467
You actually
are getting inducted.

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:30.167
-I know. I have to say, though.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.400
So, I have two boys, and they're
16 and 13, which you know.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.434
-Yeah.
-And right now I'm sort of --

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.167
You know, I'm sort of
on their nerves a lot

00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:40.667
and I get a lot
of eye rolling and stuff.

00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:42.601
But the Rock and Roll
Hall of Fame?

00:00:42.601 --> 00:00:44.334
My 13-year-old was like,

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:47.300
"So is that like
the Baseball Hall of Fame

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.801
or like the NBA Hall of Fame?"

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:50.634
And I was like, "Yeah."
And he's like...

00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:53.300
-That's pretty cool.
-And I was like...

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.300
-"Yes! I scored!
I finally did something cool!"

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.534
-I did it! Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:57.534 --> 00:01:00.934
-Do they -- Do they know --
I know they know you as mom.

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:02.501
But do they know you
as Sheryl Crow?

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.067
-They have grown up
on the tour bus.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:06.167
In fact, Wyatt took
his first steps

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.400
in the green room at "Ellen."
-That's cool.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.334
-But they don't -- I mean,
that was normal for them.

00:01:10.334 --> 00:01:12.234
They don't really understand.
But Levi the other day

00:01:12.234 --> 00:01:15.701
did say, "Mom, were you like
Taylor in your day?"

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:18.667
And I was just like,
"Oh, yeah, I was -- I was --

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:20.801
I was just like Taylor
in my day."

00:01:20.801 --> 00:01:22.434
-Yeah. You were like Taylor.
Yeah.

00:01:22.434 --> 00:01:24.434
You definitely opened the door
for Taylor, too, as well.

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:27.501
-Oh, that's very nice.
Oh, my God. That's so nice.

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:29.300
-You have a speech ready to go?

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.567
-I do.
I think it's gonna come in

00:01:31.567 --> 00:01:33.601
right around 4.5 hours.

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:35.601
-[ Laughs ]
You're thanking everybody?

00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:37.200
-Everybody I know. In fact,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.067
I want to thank the Roots
and I want to thank you.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.267
-Yeah. Of course. Thank us.
Thank the Roots.

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:44.634
Can I go through the list?

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:47.501
Missy Elliott,
Rage Against the Machine,

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:50.601
Kate Bush, the Spinners,
Willie Nelson.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:52.200
You're on the same year
as Willie Nelson.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.767
-Oh, my God. For me, that's,
like, the biggest thing.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:56.968
I mean, I am so happy
to be in the Rock Hall,

00:01:56.968 --> 00:01:58.767
and I can't believe 37 years

00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.100
of being in this business
has gone by.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.634
But I can't believe I've gotten
to sing with Willie Nelson

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.834
and I know him
and he knows his kids.

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:08.167
-You're friends with Willie.
-He offered my dad weed.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:12.167
-No! Don't do that.
Day, say no. Just say no. Yeah.

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:13.634
-It was the only time --

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:17.801
My dad is, like, you know,
great small-town attorney.

00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:19.567
Atticus Finch.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.033
And so I take him
to "Crossroads,"

00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:22.934
and Willie's, like,
offering him weed,

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:25.801
and he's like --
He was kind of like, "No."

00:02:25.801 --> 00:02:28.167
And all of a sudden, I'm like,
"Whoa. Cool guy up here."

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:30.133
You know, I was expecting him
to throw Willie

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:31.834
under, like, citizen's arrest.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:33.834
"Don't you smoke grass!"
-That's right.

00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:35.601
-Yeah.
-"I'm taking you to jail!"

00:02:35.601 --> 00:02:38.200
-Yeah. But you're performing
with Olivia Rodrigo.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.467
That's one of the surprises
in the thing.

00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:42.300
Sorry I just ruined it.
-Surprise!

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.100
-Surprise! Act surprised.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.033
When you're watching -- when
you're streaming tomorrow,

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.467
act surprised.

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:50.200
But you recently sang with
Olivia Rodrigo in Nashville.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.834
-Yeah, at the Bluebird Cafe.

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:53.968
Yeah, she is --
-Real deal.

00:02:53.968 --> 00:02:56.167
-She's the real deal.
She's precious.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:58.934
She's a great songwriter.
She's --

00:02:58.934 --> 00:03:01.000
I mean, she seems
kind of unaffected

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.234
by all of it, you know?

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:03.467
-She's handling it well.

00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:06.667
-Yeah, she is.
I mean, when I was 19, her age,

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:08.267
I was like,
"How do you fill out

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:10.067
this application for college?"
You know, I mean...

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:11.534
-How do you do
any of this stuff?

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:13.200
How did you guys meet?

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.734
-I met her last year during
a whole bunch of Grammy stuff.

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:18.167
-Yeah.
-And we wound up

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.000
on some stuff together, and...

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.033
She's super cool.
And so she asked me

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.767
to do this thing
when she came to Nashville.

00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:26.501
And so I was like, "Okay."
-Yeah.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.834
-So I texted her and said,
"Hey, would you do
the Rock Hall with me?"

00:03:28.834 --> 00:03:31.100
And she's like, "I would
love to. I'd be so honored."

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.834
-That's gonna be
an unbelievable night.

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:34.267
-I know.
I was cool with my kids.

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:36.868
-Yeah. So now you're double
cool. Now you're double cool.

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.334
-Yeah.
-Well, talk to me about this.

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:39.734
You have a new album coming out.
-I do.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:41.100
-You're debuting
the first song...

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.701
-It's full of hits.
-...tonight.

00:03:42.701 --> 00:03:44.200
-I'm kidding.
-It is.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.534
-No. I said a few years ago
I'd never make another record.

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:49.868
-You did? You might have said it
on our show or something.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.501
Or we discussed it because --
I go,

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:53.267
"Why wouldn't you
make another album?"

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:54.701
And you --
-I don't know.

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.400
I can't really trust
what comes out of my mouth.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.801
-So funny. You're so funny.
Because you were like,

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:00.901
"I don't know if people listen
to albums anymore."

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:02.467
-They don't really
listen to albums,

00:04:02.467 --> 00:04:04.968
but my philosophy about it was,

00:04:04.968 --> 00:04:07.334
okay, I'm gonna make --
I'm gonna write songs

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:09.434
that are meaningful
in the moment, right?

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:11.367
So instead of writing
an entire book,

00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:13.534
I'll just put out sentences
or I'll put, you know --

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.133
But I wound up
with a bunch of songs

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:18.234
that I'd written just recently,
starting off with "Evolution,"

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:20.934
which is a song about A.I.,
speaking about the Beatles.

00:04:20.934 --> 00:04:22.200
-Yeah.
-And it's been

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.567
so disturbing to me.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:25.601
I don't know how you
musicians feel about it,

00:04:25.601 --> 00:04:27.734
but I did a session
the other day,

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.367
and this young songwriter
had this incredible song,

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:32.267
but she needed a guy
to sing on it

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:35.067
so that she could pitch it
to male singers in Nashville.

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:38.567
Paid $5,
put in John Mayer's name,

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:40.701
and she played it for me,
and my -- [Imitates explosion]

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:44.934
There's no way you could
tell the difference.

00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:47.200
And it just blew my mind.
-It sounded like John Mayer?

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.634
-It sounded not just --
Didn't just sound like him.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:52.367
I mean, like,
his inflections and...

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:54.767
And so this is what A.I. can do.
-That is scary, right?

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:56.534
-And it really --
it really scared me.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:59.968
Only because, you know,
for me, art is like soul.

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.534
It's attached to the soul.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:05.000
So when you get into something
that's so much more advanced

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
than our brains are
at this point,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.200
it takes the soul out of it,
you know?

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.400
-It's kind of scary.
-It's scary.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:11.868
So that's how it started.

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:13.434
I wrote a song called
"Evolution" about it.

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:15.200
I called my friend
Mike Elizondo,

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.701
which was like
calling Martin Scorsese.

00:05:17.701 --> 00:05:20.300
And I said, "I have this song."
And, man, he created

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.901
the most incredible movie
around this song.

00:05:22.901 --> 00:05:24.000
-He crushed it.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.400
-He cruh-huh-hushed it!
-Yes!

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.634
-I mean, it's so amazing.

00:05:27.634 --> 00:05:30.300
So then I was like, "Hmm.
I'm gonna do some more songs."

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.000
-Yeah, yeah, you got the bug,
and you're like,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:32.968
"Come on. I'm Sheryl Crow."

00:05:32.968 --> 00:05:34.367
-"I don't need to
produce myself.

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.334
I don't have time for that."
-Yeah. Right?

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:38.167
-The album's gonna come out
March 29th.

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.334
So what you're gonna do
is release a song every month?

00:05:40.334 --> 00:05:41.901
-"Alarm Clock"
is coming out first.

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:44.033
-"Alarm Clock." Alright.
That's the one to stream.

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.501
-And every month,
we'll put out a different song.

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:47.968
-Do your boys
like "Alarm Clock"?

00:05:47.968 --> 00:05:50.067
-They really like it.
There's one on there, though,

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:52.434
that's called "Broken Record,"
and they're like,

00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:55.200
"Mom, you're not gonna put
that on your record, are you?"

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.767
And I was like,
"Yeah, yeah, I am."

00:05:56.767 --> 00:05:58.968
They're like,
"Mom, no. Just no."

00:05:58.968 --> 00:06:01.467
Do you ever get "Just no"?
-"Just no."

00:06:01.467 --> 00:06:03.667
-You ever get "Just no"
from your kids? "Just no."

00:06:03.667 --> 00:06:06.634
-My kids are 10 and 8.
They still love me.

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:08.601
-They do?
-Yeah.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.701
-Oh, that's gonna change.
-It's gonna change.

00:06:10.701 --> 00:06:12.467
-That's gonna change.
-Yeah. But that's pretty --

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:14.400
And they're coming out.
The whole family's coming out.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.734
-Yeah, and they are actually.
They're awesome. I'm so blessed.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:20.167
-Yeah. Congrats. It couldn't
happen to a better person.

00:06:20.167 --> 00:06:22.067
You're in the Rock and Roll
Hall of Fame.

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:24.300
Next time you're here,
you're gonna be ind-- Yeah!

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.601
That's exactly right!
The best!

00:06:26.601 --> 00:06:28.734
Sheryl Crow, everybody!

00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:31.767
You can live-stream the Rock
and Roll Hall of Fame induction

00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:33.033
on Disney+.

00:06:33.033 --> 00:06:34.634
And "Evolution"
will be out next year.

00:06:34.634 --> 00:06:37.133
We'll be right back with more
"Tonight Show." Stick around!

